.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception
.Dd October 28, 2026
.Dt NQRAND 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nqrand ,
.Nm nqsetrng
.Nd random bytes from the operating system or the caller's source
.Sh LIBRARY
libnoqtis, \-lnoqtis
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In noqtis.h
.Ft int
.Fn nqrand "uint8_t *buf" "size_t len"
.Vt typedef int (*nqrngfn)(uint8_t *buf, size_t len, void *user);
.Ft int
.Fn nqsetrng "nqrngfn fn" "void *user"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn nqrand
fills
.Fa buf
with
.Fa len
bytes from the operating system's cryptographically secure random source:
.Xr getrandom 2
on Linux,
.Xr arc4random_buf 3
on macOS and the BSDs,
.Fn BCryptGenRandom
on Windows, and
.Xr getentropy 3
under WebAssembly (WebCrypto
.Fn getRandomValues
with emscripten, WASI
.Fn random_get
with wasi-sdk).
Large requests are made in chunks (256 bytes for
.Xr getentropy 3 ,
256 MiB for
.Fn BCryptGenRandom ) .
On Linux,
.Xr getrandom 2
is called with no flags, so it blocks until the kernel's pool is
initialised, and a call interrupted by a signal is repeated;
.Xr arc4random_buf 3
cannot fail.
On other systems the library does not build.
There is no fallback source and no user-space generator: when the
operating system's source fails, so does
.Fn nqrand .
.Pp
Every function of
.Xr noqtis 3
that needs randomness (key generation, hedged signing, encapsulation,
mask shares) takes it from here.
.Pp
.Fn nqsetrng
makes
.Fn nqrand
take its bytes from
.Fa fn
instead, called with
.Fa user ;
.Fa fn
returns 0 when it filled
.Fa buf
and anything else when it failed, and
.Fn nqrand
then fails with
.Er NQERNG .
A
.Dv NULL
.Fa fn
gives the operating system's source back.
The setting is global.
It may be changed at any time, also while other threads call into the
library: a call draws from the source of before or of after, each with
its own
.Fa user .
The source must be a cryptographically secure one (a hardware random
number generator, a DRBG seeded from one); the library does not test it.
.Pp
In the freestanding build
.Pq Fl DNQFREE=ON ,
for firmware and kernels without an operating system's source,
.Fn nqrand
fails with
.Er NQERNG
until
.Fn nqsetrng
has given it one.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Bl -tag -width NQERNG
.It Dv NQOK
.Fa buf
holds
.Fa len
random bytes.
A
.Fa len
of 0 succeeds and writes nothing (a caller's source is still called,
with
.Fa len
0).
.It Bq Er NQEARG
.Fa buf
is
.Dv NULL
and
.Fa len
is not 0.
.It Bq Er NQERNG
The operating system's source failed, or the caller's source did, or (in
the freestanding build) no source was set.
.El
.Pp
After
.Er NQERNG ,
.Fa buf
may already hold some bytes from the source; they must not be used.
.Pp
.Fn nqsetrng
returns
.Dv NQOK .
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
uint8_t key[32];
if (nqrand(key, sizeof key) != NQOK)
  abort();
/* ... use the key ... */
nqwipe(key, sizeof key);
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr noqtis 3 ,
.Xr nqwipe 3
